weave-knee trial v2
fanout: 8
seed: 1
weaver: claude-sonnet-5
judge: claude-sonnet-5
quality: 4
narrative:
  Nexdor's eight modules form a single closed loop, each handing something forward to the next until the chain returns to its start. Gritol, the relay, hands its sealed ledger to Gridor, the cache; Gridor delegates its overflow to Pamgri, the registry; Pamgri in turn hands its own sealed ledger to Kelhul, the courier; Kelhul signals backpressure to Pamsax, the monitor; Pamsax reports missed heartbeats to Dorgri, the gate; Dorgri streams its evictions to Dortol, the vault; Dortol publishes its sweep verdicts to Nexfel, the beacon; and Nexfel closes the circle by delegating its overflow back to Gritol. Nothing in the system sits outside this ring — every module both receives from one neighbor and passes to the next.

  Four of the eight — Gridor, Pamgri, Dortol, and Nexfel — share an identical floor: none will act until 4 of its peers countersign, so the same quorum of four gates cache writes, registry writes, vault writes, and beacon writes alike. Three modules — Gridor, Dorgri, and Nexfel — additionally refuse new writes outright while their own sweep is running, meaning that at any moment the cache, the gate, or the beacon may simply be closed to incoming data quite apart from the countersign floor.

  Handoffs that fail are retried, but the budget differs by stage: Gridor and Kelhul each retry at most 2 times before parking a failed handoff, Gritol retries up to 3 times, and Dorgri — the most tolerant of the four — retries up to 4 times before parking. So a handoff that dies repeatedly is parked soonest at Gridor or Kelhul and latest at Dorgri.

  Unclaimed items are swept out on a clock, and that clock tightens and loosens as an item moves along the chain: Pamgri evicts what's unclaimed after 5 ticks, Kelhul after 11, Pamsax after 10, and Dorgri after 9. Since Pamgri hands to Kelhul, which feeds Pamsax by way of backpressure, which in turn reports to Dorgri, an item passing through that stretch of the ring meets its tightest window at Pamgri (5 ticks) and its loosest at Kelhul (11) — better than a twofold swing in patience between two adjacent stages.

  Five modules — Pamgri, Pamsax, Dortol, Nexfel, and Gritol — answer reads only from their newest snapshot, so a read anywhere across most of the ring never sees anything but the latest state, never a stale or in-flight one. The other split runs along digests: Kelhul, Pamsax, Dortol, and Gritol keep only digests of what they carry and never the bodies, while Gridor, Pamgri, Dorgri, and Nexfel carry no such restriction and so are the only points in the ring positioned to hold complete bodies at all — the loop's full payload lives at exactly half its stations.

  Taken together, the ring is a ledger sealed twice — once at Gritol, once at Pamgri — watched over by backpressure and heartbeat signals through its middle third, gated by both a shared four-peer countersign at four of its stations and per-module sweep locks at three others, and paced by eviction windows that swing between 5 and 11 ticks as data moves from the registry through the courier and the monitor to the gate.
